.\" page (this project's own text), so it carries no third-party
.\" notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as cksum.1/paste.1.
.\" Rendered at build time to build/man/SPLIT.TXT and staged on the
.\" play image as /usr/man/SPLIT: `more /usr/man/SPLIT`.
.TH SPLIT 1 "September 15, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
split \- split a file into pieces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B split
[
.B \-l
.I n
|
.B \-b
.I n[k|m]
] [
.B \-a
.I suffix_length
] [
.I file
[
.I name
]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Split
reads
.I file
(standard input if it is absent or
.RB " " \- )
and writes it out in pieces, each a separate file named
.I name
(default
.BR x )
followed by a generated suffix of
.I suffix_length
letters
.RB ( \-a ,
default 2):
.BR aa ,
.BR ab ,
and so on. The default is
.B \-l
.B 1000
(1000 lines per piece).
.B \-b
.I n
splits by raw byte count instead; a piece boundary may fall in the
middle of a line, and the bytes on either side of it are copied
verbatim.
.I n
may be suffixed
.B k
(1024) or
.B m
(1,048,576, KNOWN LIMITATION: the result silently wraps modulo
65536 on this 16-bit machine).
.PP
A source whose length divides evenly by the split unit never
leaves a trailing empty piece; an empty source still gets exactly
one empty piece. If
.I name
plus
.I suffix_length
would exceed ProDOS's own 15-character filename limit, or more
pieces are needed than
.I suffix_length
letters can name, the run stops with a diagnostic and exit 1 --
every piece already written stays.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/SPLIT  this page
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success; 1 if the input file could not be
opened, the name/suffix length exceeds 15 characters, an output
piece could not be created or written, or the suffix letters are
exhausted; 2 on a usage error.
.SH BUGS
None known.
